1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Wang Huzhi 王胡之 (ca. 320–ca. 349), ''zi'' Xiuling 修齡, Eastern Jin poet and ''xuanxue'' thinker.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wang Huzhi's ancestral home was Linyi 臨沂 in Langye 琅邪 commandery (modern Linyi, Shandong). He was the son of Wang Yi 王廙 (274–322), brother-in-law of Emperor Yuan (r. 317–322) and uncle of Wang Xizhi 王羲之 (303–361). In 322, Wang Dun
2 CONC  王敦 (266–324) ordered Wang Yi to assassinate Sima Cheng 司馬承 (264–322). Sima Cheng's son Sima Wuji 司馬無忌 (d. 350) was acquainted with Wang Huzhi in their youth. After learning that Wang Huzhi's father had killed Sima Cheng, Sima Wuj
2 CONC i resolved to exact vengeance by killing Wang Huzhi. Ca. 335, Wang Huzhi fled to Jingzhou 荊州 (administrative seat, Jiangling), where he joined the staff of Yu Liang 庾亮 (389–340), who assumed the post of regional inspector of Jingz
2 CONC hou, Jiangzhou 江州 (administrative seat, Nanchang), and Yuzhou 豫州 in 334. On one occasion in Wuchang 武昌, on an autumn day, Yu Liang had Wang Huzhi, Yin Hao 殷浩 (d. 362), and other members of his staff climb the southern tower and re
2 CONC cite poetry. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ca. 341, Wang Huzhi was assigned as governor of Nanping 南平 (administrative seat, northeast of modern Lanshan 藍山, Hunan). Ca. 343, he was appointed senior administrator to Chu Pou 褚裒 (303–349), who was the father of Chu Suanzi 褚蒜子 
2 CONC (324–384), empress of Emperor Kang (342– 344). Emperor Kang died on 17 November 344, and Empress Kang was named Empress Dowager on 22 November. She was de facto regent for the two-year-old Emperor Mu (r. 344–361). Empress Kang wis
2 CONC hed to place all authority in the hands of Chu Pou, but Wang Huzhi convinced Chu Pou to yield to Sima Yu 司馬昱 (321–372), the future Emperor Jianwen (r. 371–372). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ca. 347, Wang Huzhi was assigned to the post of governor of Wuxing 吳興 (administrative seat, Wucheng 烏程, south of modern Wuxing, Zhejiang). Ca. 349, he was called to the imperial court where he was appointed palace attendant. He wa
2 CONC s also appointed governor of Danyang 丹陽, which was the administrative area that included the capital Jiankang, and director of the palace library. He did not take up any of these positions. In 349, Shi Hu 石虎 (295–349), ruler of th
2 CONC e Later Zhao state, died. The Eastern Jin wished to take advantage of a succession struggle at the Later Zhao court to launch an expedition to recover Luoyang. Wang Huzhi was named leader of court gentleman of the west and regiona
2 CONC l inspector of Sizhou 司州 (administrative seat, northeast of modern Luoyang) with the assignment of leading the army on the northern campaign. Wang Huzhi had suffered from epilepsy most of his life, and he suffered a serious attack
2 CONC  and tried to resign. The court did not accept his resignation. He died before he could begin the journey. He was about thirty years of age at the time. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The monograph on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'' lists Wang Huzhi's collected works in ten ''juan''. This was lost already in the Tang. Lu Qinli collects two of his poems in ''Xian Qin Han Wei Jin Nanbeichao shi''. Yan Kejun inclu
2 CONC des four of his prose pieces in ''Quan shanggu Sandai Qin Han Sanguo Liuchao wen''.
2 CONT
2 CONT David R. Knechtges
2 CONT
2 CONT Citation
2 CONT * 王胡之 Wang Huzhi, Brill’s Chinese Reference Shelf. Brill Online, 2017. http://chinesereferenceshelf.brillonline.com/ancient-literature/entries/COM-0164
