1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Wang Sengda''' 王僧達 (423–458), Liu-Song period writer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wang Sengda’s ancestral home was Linyi 臨沂 in Langye 琅邪 commndery. He was the youngest son of Wang Hong 王弘 (379–432), who was one of the most distinguished court officials at the beginning of the Song dynasty. Emperor Wen had heard
2 CONC  that Wang Sengda was precocious, and he summoned him to an interview in the Deyang Hall. He was so impressed with his learning, he arranged to have him married to the daughter of Liu Yiqing 劉義慶 (403–444), Prince of Linchuan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wang Sengda began his official career in 441 as a military aide to Liu Jun 劉濬 (429–453), Prince of Shixing 始興. He was then promoted to secretary on the staff of the heir designate. However, Wang Sengda spent much of his time watch
2 CONC ing fighting ducks, flying falcons, and racing hounds. He also liked to butcher cows. Claiming his family was poor, he requested to be assigned to a commandery post. Emperor Wen recommended that he be assigned to Qin commandery 秦郡
2 CONC  (administrative seat north of modern Luhe 六合, Jiangsu), but the director of the Ministry of Personnel, Yu Bingzhi 庾炳之 (388–45) objected on the grounds that “Sengda was not capable of overseeing the people.” The emperor decided no
2 CONC t to appoint him to any commandery post.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wang Sengda was then promoted to librarian in the editorial service of the heir designate. However, he soon had to resign his post to observe mourning for his mother. At the conclusion of the mourning period, Wang Sengda was appoi
2 CONC nted governor of Xuancheng 宣城 (modern Xuancheng, Anhui). Instead of attending to his duties, he spent much of his time hunting and going out on rambles for days at a time. He often heard cases in the hunting grounds.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 448, the Northern Wei army invaded the area north of the Yangzi River. Wang Sengda volunteered to join in the defense of the capital. After the Wei army withdrew, Wang returned to his post in Xuancheng. Soon thereafter he was t
2 CONC ransferred to the position of governor of Yixing 義興 (modern Yixing 宜興, Jiangsu).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 453, when Liu Jun 劉駿 (430–464), the future Emperor Xiaowu (r. 453–464), led his troops to attack his older brother Liu Shao 劉劭 (ca. 426–453), who murdered his father, Emperor Wen, Wang Sengda went south to welcome Liu Jun. Liu 
2 CONC Jun appointed Wang Sengda to high-ranking positions at court when Liu Jun ascended the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Owing to his distinguished family background and vast learning, Wang Sengda acted in an overbearing, imperious manner that offended Emperor Xiaowu. Emperor Xiaowu punished him with five demotions in one year. He then had him assig
2 CONC ned as governor of Wu commandery 吳郡 (modern Suzhou). While serving in this post he expropriated large sums of money from wealthy Buddhist monks. In 456, Wang Sengda returned to the court as chamberlain for ceremonials. He also was
2 CONC  removed from this position because of insubordination, but he was soon appointed governor of Linhuai 臨淮 (modern Xuyi 眙貽, Jiangsu).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 457, Wang Sengda returned to the imperial court as general of the guards and supervisor of the palace cadets in the household of the crown prince. He also was awarded the title Marquis of Ningling 寧陵 county. In 458, he was prom
2 CONC oted to secretariat director.
2 CONT
2 CONT During this time Wang Sengda offended Lu Qiongzhi 路瓊之, the nephew of Empress Dowager Lu 路太后 (412–466), and was accused of planning a coup against the imperial house. He was allowed to commit suicide in prison. Emperor Xiaowu perso
2 CONC nally wrote the biography of Wang Sengda for the Song history that was being compiled during that time.
2 CONT
2 CONT The monographs on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'', the two Tang histories, and the ''Song shi'' list Wang Sengda’s collected works in ten ''juan''. This was lost in the Yuan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wang Sengda was an accomplished poet. Zhong Rong places his verse in the middle grade of the ''Shi pin''. One of his best known compositions is “Ji Yan Guanglu wen” 祭顏光祿文 (Offering for Grand Master for Splendid Happiness Yan), whi
2 CONC ch he wrote upon the death of Yan Yanzhi 顏延之 (384–456). Yan Kejun collected seven of his prose pieces in ''Quan shanggu Sandai Qin Han Sanguo Liuchao wen''. Lu Qinli preserves six of his poems in ''Xian Qin Han Wei Jin Nanbei chao
2 CONC  shi''.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wu Jie, David R. Knechtges
2 CONT
2 CONT Citation
2 CONT * 王僧達 Wang Sengda, Brill’s Chinese Reference Shelf. Brill Online, 2017. http://chinesereferenceshelf.brillonline.com/ancient-literature/entries/COM-0190
