1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Wang Gui 王規 (492–536), ''zi'' Weiming 威明, Liang period writer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wang Gui’s ancestral home was Linyi 臨沂 in Langye 琅邪 (modern Linyi, Shandong). He was the grandson of Wang Jian 王儉 (452–489), and the father of Wang Bao 王褒 (ca. 511–ca. 575). His father Wang Qian 王騫 (474–522) held high office in th
2 CONC e Southern Qi and Liang.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wang Gui had obtained a thorough knowledge of the Five Classics by the age of twelve. He began his official career as assistant in the palace library. In 512, he was assigned as secretary on the staff of the crown prince Xiao Tong
2 CONC  蕭統 (501–531). In 513, when the Taiji dian 太極殿 (Grand Ultimate Hall) was rebuilt, Wang Gui presented the “Xindian fu” 新殿賦 (''Fu'' on the new hall) to celebrate the occasion. He served briefly as vice director of the palace library
2 CONC  and then returned to Xiao Tong’s staff as secretary. In 521, when Xiao Gang 蕭綱 (503–551) was appointed regional inspector of Southern Xuzhou, Wang Gui was assigned as an aide and adviser. In 522, while Wang Gui was serving as gov
2 CONC ernor of Xin’an 新安 (administrative seat Xin 新 county, west of modern Chun’an 淳安, Zhejiang), his father Wang Qian died, and he resigned office to observe mourning for him.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the mourning period, Wang Gui inherited his father’s title of Marquis of Nanchang county. In 525, Emperor Wu issued an edict commanding him, Yin Yun 殷芸 (471–529), Wang Xi 王錫 (499–534), Zhang Mian 張緬 (490–531) and others to s
2 CONC erve Xiao Tong in the Eastern Palace. About this same time, Xiao Yi 蕭繹 (508–555) served as governor of the capital area. He hosted a banquet for a group of courtiers, including Wang Gui. He ordered Wang Gui to conduct a ''jiu ling
2 CONC '' 酒令, a drinking bout that involved the composition of witty poetic lines. Wang Gui replied, “Ever since the Jin house came south, there has never been this kind of activity.” Xiao Chen 蕭琛 (ca. 465–531) and Fu Zhao 傅昭 (454–428), 
2 CONC who were in attendance, praised him for his astute remark. In 525, Emperor Wu hosted a farewell banquet in the Wende Hall in honor of Yuan Jinglong 元景隆 (fl. 525–545), who was about to depart on a long journey to Guangzhou. He orde
2 CONC red all of the courtiers present to compose a poem in fifty couplets. Wang Gui quickly dashed off a piece that won extreme praise from the emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wang Gui probably only served two or three months in the Eastern Palace. He was then promoted to palace attendant. In 530, Xiao Gang was named regional inspector of Yangzhou 揚州, the province that included the capital. Wang Gui was
2 CONC  appointed a senior administrator on his staff. Xiao Tong died suddenly in May 531. Emperor Wu selected Xiao Gang as heir designate. He was granted this title on 27 June 531. Soon thereafter Wang Gui was appointed palace cadet in 
2 CONC the crown prince’s household. He seems to have had a close relationship with Xiao Gang.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wang Gui some time later was sent out as governor of Wujun 吳郡 (modern Suzhou). During his tenure in this position, he offended local powerful men, who tried to have him impeached. Wang Gui returned to the imperial court as directo
2 CONC r of the Census Section in the Department of State Affairs. He soon received an offer to return to his former post of palace cadet on Xiao Gang’s staff, but he declined the position on the grounds of illness. Wang Gui built a vill
2 CONC a on Zhongshan 鍾山 (Zhong Mountain) east of the capital, were he died in 536 at the age of forty-five.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Wang Gui’s biography in the ''Liang shu'', his collected writings consisted of twenty ''juan''. However, it was lost already before the Tang. He also wrote a commentary to the ''Xu Han shu'' 續漢書 of Sima Biao 司馬彪 (ca. 
2 CONC 240–ca. 306) in two hundred ''juan''. This also was lost before the Tang. His only extant works are two quatrains titled “Dayan” 大言 (Magniloquence) and “Xiaoyan” 小言 (Diminiloquence) that he wrote to match pieces by the same title 
2 CONC by Xiao Tong.
2 CONT
2 CONT David R. Knechtges
