3 CTRY United States
1 BURI
2 DATE 1919
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ming Mow Chin, birth name Wong, (means prosperous) (1856-1918) was the eldest of three sons of Mun Get Chin. The family owned and farmed rice paddies. After primary school in the village he was encouraged to study 
2 CONC herbal medicine by apprenticing in a city firm for 3 years. He was a studious person and later earned the first degree (title) conferred under the former civil service examination system in the Ching Dynasty. In 1872 he married To
2 CONC y Liu from a nearby village called Poo Tou Yang. Toy Liu had bound feet and known to be quite an elegant matriarch. She was addressed as Madam for her husband's title.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ming Mow and Toy had two children, a son Gem Wah, (Wah Chin) born in 1876 and a daughter Sem Guey (married Moy) born in 1878.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1878 Ming Mow came to the U.S. as a Chinese merchant and peddled herbs and patent formulas at railroad camps in the Western states. In 1883 he returned to the village for 4 years when he bought land and built a house for the fa
2 CONC mily. When he re entered the U.S. in 1887 he decided to settle in Boston Chinatown. After a year of partnering with friends and working for two small stores he started his own store named Wing On Tank and Company (English translat
2 CONC ion as Lasting Peace Company) at 38 Harrison Avenue with a storefront and a basement for storage. In 1891 he took a 14 month trip to Hong Kong and China to purchase merchandise and to socialize with friends who owned export compan
2 CONC ies.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1905 Ming Mow seeing his son Wah Chin had an established family he petitioned for his admittance to the U.S. as a son of a domiciled merchant. With the help of his son he moved to a larger storefront at 10 Oxford Street in 1909
2 CONC . At the same time also leased the building at 32 Oxford Street for sublets to friends and relatives.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1914 Wah Chin went to China and had three more children, a 3rd daughter, Fung Nui born in 1915, a son Yoke Gue in 1917 and a 3rd son Kwok Gee in 1918. Meanwhile, Ming Mow died of a stroke at 10 Oxford Street on January 10, 1918
2 CONC . Wah Chin hastened to return to the U.S. in March 1918 to bury his father at the Mt. Hope cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ming Mow's remain was later shipped back to China by the City according to the regulations of the time, and before the war involving the part of our area, and re-entombed at a hillside grave near the Chung Ling Village.
